            <h2 class="comments-header">2 Comments</h2>
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-40" class="comment">
                <p><strong>Michael, <a href="https://thebaseballchronicle.com/essays/defending_the_indefensible#comment-40" title="Comment permalink">Jun 24, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>PEDs - given that 99.999% of sports reporters don't even know how they work, much less how many were taking them, when they were taking them, and what players were taking pre-Canseco's book - are not "indefensible." Players, from Ty Cobb and his sharpened spikes to Gaylord Perry's well-known ball doctoring to Carl Everett's feet outside the batter's box, have always "cheated." </p>

<p>Punching one's wife in the face, in view of witnesses, then getting a free pass from the media, some of whom like Jack McCaffery scold the FANS for not letting it go just one day after the player returns - now that's indefensible.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-41" class="comment">
                <p><strong><a title="https://pitchersandpoets.com" href="https://pitchersandpoets.com" rel="nofollow">Ted</a>, <a href="https://thebaseballchronicle.com/essays/defending_the_indefensible#comment-41" title="Comment permalink">Jul  2, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>There is fun cheating, and there's nasty cheating.</p>

<p>Scuffing the ball, King Kelly skipping second, scooting outside of the batter's box: each of these has the playful element akin to the pro wrestler pointing over the ref's shoulder then clanging a chair over his opponent's head.</p>

<p>Nasty cheating includes gambling--which profits criminals and involves lots of scheming and losing on purpose (a far greater crime than cheating to win)--and PEDs.</p>

<p>PEDs are on another plane, as I think fans find it fundamentally disturbing to toy with chemical balances and hormonal what-have-yous. It's creepy, and as Michael points out, nobody understands it. For that reason, to me, anyway, there's none of the charm of the earlier cheating mentions, only, rather, the image of that creepy egomaniac at the local gym with the neon-trimmed bike shorts and the hair plugs, squatting 1000 pounds and smooching his biceps in the mirror.</p>

            </div>
